<div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;margin-bottom:16px"><span style="padding:2px 10px;border-radius:4px;background:rgba(244,114,182,.15);color:#f9a8d4;font-size:.7em;font-weight:700">🔬 Scientist</span><span style="font-size:.78em;color:var(--text-muted)">Pure & applied mathematics</span></div>
<h2>Optimization Theory — The Science of Best Choices</h2>
<p style="font-size:.95em;color:var(--text-sec);margin-bottom:16px">Optimization is the mathematics of making the best possible choice under constraints. It spans linear programming (simplex method), convex optimization (gradient descent, Newton), nonlinear programming (KKT conditions), integer programming (branch-and-bound), and stochastic optimization (simulated annealing, evolutionary algorithms). The subject is the foundation of operations research, machine learning, engineering design, and economics.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"Optimization theory is the mathematical art of making the best of things."</div><div class="attribution">— George Dantzig</div></div>

<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>📐 Linear Programming — The Simplex Method and Duality</h2>
<div class="story-card">
<div class="story-label">Optimal Allocation</div>
<div class="story-title">Linear Programming — Maximizing Under Linear Constraints</div>
<div class="story-text">A linear program (LP) minimizes cᵀx subject to Ax = b, x ≥ 0. The feasible region is a convex polytope. The <strong>simplex method</strong> (Dantzig, 1947) walks along edges of the polytope from vertex to vertex, decreasing the objective at each step — exponential worst-case but polynomial in practice. <strong>Strong duality</strong>: if the primal (min cᵀx) has an optimal solution x*, then the dual (max bᵀy) has an optimal y* with cᵀx* = bᵀy*. Complementary slackness gives optimality conditions. <strong>Interior point methods</strong> (Karmarkar, 1984) traverse the interior of the feasible region using barrier functions — polynomial time O(n³L). <strong>Integer linear programming</strong> (ILP) adds integrality constraints — much harder (NP-complete). The <strong>branch-and-bound</strong> algorithm solves ILPs by recursively partitioning the feasible region and bounding the objective.</div>
<div class="story-moral">Linear programming duality is the deepest idea — every maximization problem has a matching minimization problem with the same optimal value.</div>
</div>

<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🔄 Convex Optimization — Gradient Descent and Newton's Method</h2>
<div class="story-card">
<div class="story-label">Descent Direction</div>
<div class="story-title">Convex Optimization — When Local Minima Are Global</div>
<div class="story-text">A set is <strong>convex</strong> if the line between any two points lies in it. A function f is convex if f(θx+(1−θ)y) ≤ θf(x)+(1−θ)f(y) — a convex function on a convex set has every local minimum be global. <strong>Gradient descent</strong> x_{k+1} = x_k − α∇f(x_k) converges to optimum for convex f with Lipschitz gradient — O(1/k) rate. <strong>Stochastic gradient descent</strong> (SGD) uses noisy gradient estimates ∇f_i(x_k) instead of full gradient — the workhorse of machine learning. <strong>Newton's method</strong> x_{k+1} = x_k − [∇²f(x_k)]^{-1}∇f(x_k) converges quadratically near optimum but costs O(n³) per step. <strong>BFGS</strong> and <strong>Limited-memory BFGS</strong> (L-BFGS) approximate the Hessian with gradient differences — quasi-Newton methods balance cost and convergence speed. <strong>Nesterov accelerated gradient</strong> achieves O(1/k²) convergence for smooth convex functions — optimal first-order method.</div>
<div class="story-moral">Convex optimization is the golden land — every local minimum is global, and gradient methods find it reliably.</div>
</div>

<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>⚡ KKT Conditions and Constrained Nonlinear Optimization</h2>
<div class="story-card">
<div class="story-label">Optimization Under Constraints</div>
<div class="story-title">KKT Conditions — The First-Order Necessary Conditions for Optimality</div>
<div class="story-text">For minimize f(x) subject to g_i(x) ≤ 0, h_j(x) = 0, the <strong>Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) conditions</strong> are: (1) Stationarity: ∇f + Σ μ_i∇g_i + Σ λ_j∇h_j = 0, (2) Primal feasibility: g_i(x) ≤ 0, h_j(x) = 0, (3) Dual feasibility: μ_i ≥ 0, (4) Complementary slackness: μ_i g_i(x) = 0. For convex problems, KKT is both necessary and sufficient. The <strong>Lagrangian</strong> L(x,μ,λ) = f(x) + Σ μ_i g_i(x) + Σ λ_j h_j(x). The <strong>penalty method</strong> adds a penalty term for constraint violations. The <strong>augmented Lagrangian</strong> (method of multipliers) adds a quadratic penalty to improve convergence. <strong>Sequential quadratic programming</strong> (SQP) solves constrained nonlinear problems by approximating them as QP subproblems at each iteration.</div>
<div class="story-moral">The KKT conditions are the ultimate check for whether you've found the best possible point under constraints.</div>
</div>

<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🎲 Stochastic Optimization and Metaheuristics</h2>
<div class="story-card">
<div class="story-label">Randomness as a Tool</div>
<div class="story-title">When Deterministic Methods Fail — Stochastic and Global Optimization</div>
<div class="story-text">Non-convex optimization has multiple local minima — deterministic gradient methods get trapped. <strong>Simulated annealing</strong> accepts worse solutions with decreasing probability (controlled by temperature) to escape local minima — inspired by metallurgy. <strong>Genetic algorithms</strong> evolve a population of solutions through selection, crossover, and mutation — they explore the search space broadly. <strong>Particle swarm optimization</strong> (PSO) models a swarm of particles moving toward personal and global best positions. <strong>Bayesian optimization</strong> builds a probabilistic surrogate model (Gaussian process) of the objective function and uses an acquisition function (expected improvement, upper confidence bound) to choose the next evaluation point — ideal for expensive black-box functions. <strong>Evolutionary strategies</strong> (CMA-ES) adapt the covariance matrix of the search distribution for efficient exploration. For convex nondifferentiable problems, <strong>subgradient methods</strong> replace gradients with subgradients — they converge slowly but reliably.</div>
<div class="story-moral">When the landscape is rugged, randomness helps — simulated annealing, genetic algorithms, and Bayesian optimization explore in different ways.</div>
</div>

